A||N||1||FieroFanaticus2||Wheel Bearings?  Maybe Steering Problem??||||1||
Z||000000||FieroFanaticus2||08-13-2001||06:26 PM||||Ok, so when I back out of my driveway in the morning and turn the steering wheel to the right, I hear and feel something like a 'clink'.  And when I am driving somewhat fast (40-90 mph) on a country highway, right curves are smooth as silk, but left curves are kind of jerky and need to be corrected (can't just hold the wheel in one spot and stay on the correct path).  SO I got sick of it and pulled over, got out and grabbed the front drivers side wheel and shook it hard.  It moved a little back and forth and make a grinding sound.  This is also the same wheel that made a horrible 'humming' sound for a few days when the brakes where applied.  But the humming stopped after a few days by itself.<BR>Also, when driving, I sometimes turn the steer left to right and right to left quickly, much like a race car does in the beginning of a race, and it seems to sway a lot more than it should.<BR>SO, I was wondering if maybe it is the bearings in the front drivers side wheel that are possibly shot, or if it is my steering?  Would a front and/or rear sway bar help any (it's an '88 coupe)?  Or am I just screwed all the way around?<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dump_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||08-13-2001||08:51 PM||||You initial description does sound like wheel bearings (grind, whine, etc.) The bearing preload does tend to allow the wheel to move slightly normally though. <P>I would also check the upper and lower ball joints. Those can also move during a turn. You get this abrupt change in camber in the wheel. You can't check them out of the car (unless they are completely destroyed) since the internal spring is strong enough to make them feel tight without a load.<P>Make sure the grinding isn't something with the brake pad before you rip it apart.<P>Outer tie-rods too, but they don't account for the noise (and neither goes the ball joints.)<P>TK||||reg||1
